speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I should like to say that in fact as a result of the hon. member's suggestion and suggestions by a number of our colleagues in the Quebec caucus, a meeting was held in Quebec City on March 1. The meeting involved the Minister of Industry, Trade and Commerce and Minister of Regional Economic Expansion (Mr. Gray), the Minister of Energy, Mines and Resources (Mr. Lalonde) and the Minister of State for Small Businesses and Tourism (Mr. Lapointe), representing the Government of Canada, and on the other side, Ministers Landry, Parizeau and Biron. On that occasion, a senior officials liaison committee was formed with orders to report in a month's time on ways and means of harmonizing and co-ordinating business assistance programs administered by both governments.
However, I would point out to the hon. member that the federal ministers reminded their provincial counterparts that the Canadian government lends assistance to small and medium-size businesses in Quebec through the following seven instruments:
(1)	The Small Businesses Loans Act. More than 5,000 loan guarantees were authorized in Quebec in 1980-81 under this program;
(2)	The business expansion program under which 250 grants and 54 loan guarantees were approved in Quebec in 1980-81;
(3)	The Federal Business Development Bank authorized more that 1,200 loans in Quebec in 1980-81 and underwrote 13 risk capital ventures;
(4)	The program for export market development. No fewer than 477 grants were authorized in Quebec during fiscal year 1980-81;
(5)	The Department of Regional Economic Expansion legislation on industrial grants. No fewer than 612 grants were approved in Quebec in 1980-81;
(6)	The Export Development Corporation loaned $608 million to Quebec companies and provided insurance coverage on goods exported from Quebec and valued at $931 million;
(7)	The federal bond program for small and medium-size businesses enabled over 1,318 Quebec businesses to secure loans worth $186 million during the period from April, 1981, to January, 1982.
In addition to those seven instruments, the Canadian government has established in the past year, as the hon. member knows, a few programs aimed at helping businesses and workers adversely affected by foreign competition, including the industry and labour adjustment program and the Canadian Industrial Renewal Board-
